I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern range/resolution, reusable refinement utilities, and candidate databases. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test alternatives under a common profile model, and only retain phases supported by coherent diffraction evidence.

The chemistry points first to Mg–W–O reaction products, but at 700 °C unreacted MgO/WO₃ and polymorph/source-condition variants remain realistic. I’m now quantifying the peak set and inspecting the supplied analogue-search workflow before selecting records.

The peak inventory already separates two obvious families: a dense low-angle/monoclinic set and the cubic MgO sequence near 42.94°, 62.26°, 74.57°, 78.88°, and 93.82°. The 23–24.5° multiplet also requires testing residual monoclinic WO₃ against MgWO₄ overlap rather than assigning it by chemistry alone.

All major peaks now index coherently as three phases: wolframite-type MgWO₄, room-temperature monoclinic WO₃, and periclase MgO. The phase-discriminating peaks include MgWO₄ 100 at 18.879°, WO₃ 002/020/200 at 23.12/23.57/24.38°, and MgO 200 at 42.94°. I’ll carry all three into quantitative refinement and keep alternate WO₃ records on the challenge list.

I’m setting up a conservative GSAS‑II baseline with the reported Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ wavelengths and 0.5 ratio, Poisson count weights, a low-order smooth background, one specimen-displacement term, and neutral phase broadening/texture terms. Atomic coordinates and occupancies will remain fixed unless the profile gives enough independent evidence to justify changing them.

The three-phase baseline converged to roughly 57 wt% MgWO₄, 36 wt% WO₃, and 7 wt% MgO. A common nonnegative U/W/X profile reduced Rwp from 21.6% to 9.31%. Refining SH/L changed Rwp by only 0.0036 percentage points and was 97% correlated with specimen displacement, so it is currently unsupported and will be pruned. I’m inspecting residuals by isolated peak family before testing phase-specific broadening and alternate WO₃ records.

The converged no-texture reference is stable at Rwp 9.3142% with no bound/frozen parameters. Its largest errors are intensity, not peak position: MgWO₄ 100/200 are consistently underpredicted while 010 is overpredicted, a coherent multi-order signature that makes [100] preferred orientation a physically testable hypothesis. The alternate WO₃ records are still running under the identical baseline.

Record challenge results favor the neutron-derived room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ over the older X-ray record (Rwp 8.861% vs 9.314%), while orthorhombic WO₃ is decisively rejected (Rwp 11.997%, common U forced to its upper bound). A triclinic WO₃ record fits best at Rwp 8.696%, but that polymorph is normally a low-temperature form; because measurement temperature is absent, I’m retaining that symmetry assignment with explicit caution and will see whether physically supported texture/profile terms preserve the ranking.

MgWO₄ texture is strongly supported across multiple orders: an MD axis [010] lowers Rwp from 8.696% to 6.194% with a moderate parameter of 1.273; [100], [001], and diagonal alternatives are all materially worse. This is not a single-peak fix—the MgWO₄ Bragg R factor falls from 7.37% to 3.78%. I’m now challenging WO₃ texture independently while keeping that accepted MgWO₄ model fixed in form.

WO₃ texture trials all stayed within ~0.016 percentage points of the no-texture model and disagreed on axis, with MD values within about 2% of unity; that is not a robust multi-family texture signature, so WO₃ texture is rejected. MgO texture is even less identifiable: its scale and MD term correlate at 88–98% and refinements diverge, so MgO remains untextured. Next I’m comparing isotropic size and microstrain for each phase, one phase and one mechanism at a time.

The first phase-specific broadening trials are failing the physical checks: MgWO₄ size collapses to the neutral limit, while its strain trial drives both strain and the common U term into bounds and worsens the fit. I’m treating those as rejected models and letting the equivalent WO₃/MgO challenges finish.

WO₃ behaves differently: an isotropic crystallite-size term gives a large, stable improvement (Rwp 6.19% → 3.51%) at about 80 nm, with no bound hits. I’ll compare that directly against a WO₃ microstrain model before accepting it; the phase fractions move substantially, so this distinction matters.

The size/strain comparison is computationally slow because each candidate is being saved, reopened, and replayed across the full 9.9–100.1° range. That replay is intentional: it catches refinements that look favorable in one pass but are not stable when reconstructed.

WO₃ microstrain also has strong support and fits better than size alone (Rwp 2.98%, ~0.84% rms microstrain), but its replay has not yet reached the convergence threshold I want. After the MgO controls, I’ll test size plus strain together and retain only terms that stay finite, complementary, and replay-stable.

The controls are clear: neither MgWO₄ nor MgO supports a phase-specific size/strain term; those trials return to neutral limits or hit bounds. Only WO₃ needs extra broadening, so I’m now refining its size and strain jointly, then I’ll compare the competing WO₃ symmetry records under that same final nuisance model.

The joint model is now being challenged from a converged strain-only state. The strain-only solution is stable in value (about 8,340 µstrain) and fraction, though the all-parameter Hessian takes small damped steps because W and X are correlated at ~83%; there are still no bound hits or frozen parameters.

The joint WO₃ size+strain trial is rejected: it does not improve Rwp, size and strain correlate at 93.5%, and the added size parameter destabilizes convergence. I have stopped that unnecessary replay and will retain the simpler strain-only broadening model for the symmetry comparison.

The final-model symmetry challenge reverses the preliminary ranking: the room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ record (COD 2311041, P2₁/c setting) reaches Rwp 2.789% versus 2.982% for the triclinic record under the same MgWO₄ texture and WO₃ strain model. It also replays cleanly (max shift/esd 0.0046, no bounds), so monoclinic WO₃ is now the selected structure.

A coherent cross-phase high-angle intensity trend is real: refining the instrument polarization fraction improves Rwp from 2.789% to 2.392%, converging to 0.5365 ± 0.0028—close to the physically expected 0.5 for an essentially unpolarized laboratory source and far from its bounds. I’m accepting this instrument correction before judging any further texture or roughness.

The residual profile is now physically adequate across low, mid, and high angle: isolated peaks have aligned positions and comparable widths/asymmetry, the six-term background is smooth (about 3,190–3,590 counts), and no systematic hkl-dependent width pattern remains. I’m doing the required final texture re-challenge under this rebuilt profile so the earlier MgWO₄ [010] result is not carried forward uncritically.

The no-texture replay worsens Rwp from 2.392% to 7.114%, confirming that MgWO₄ texture is not a background/scale surrogate. One implementation detail in the parallel WO₃/MgO challenges initially removed that accepted MgWO₄ term; I caught it from the residual jump, stopped those two invalid branches, and restarted them with MgWO₄ [010] retained.

The rebuilt profile preserves a uniquely strong MgWO₄ [010] solution (Rwp 2.392%; alternatives tested so far are 5–7%). WO₃ shows a much smaller but stable [100] correction (MD ≈ 1.028, Rwp 2.339%); I’m finishing the diagonal-axis controls before deciding whether that modest term has enough directional support to retain.

The texture re-challenge is complete. MgWO₄ [010] is uniquely and strongly supported. WO₃ gives several small, similarly good corrections ([100], [110], [101], [111]) without a uniquely defensible axis, so I’m pruning WO₃ texture as model-dependent; MgO texture is numerically unstable and scale-coupled and is also rejected. The selected fractions are essentially unchanged by those rejected WO₃ trials.

All three phases survive the final inventory challenge. Removing MgO alone raises Rwp from 2.392% to 3.064% despite its ~7 wt% abundance; removing WO₃ or MgWO₄ raises it to 17.57% and >21%, respectively. MgO is therefore a supported weak phase, not a scale artifact. I’m doing one last fair WO₃ polymorph check with polarization refined in both models.

The triclinic replay is taking longer because its cell/profile Hessian is less well conditioned than the selected monoclinic model. I’m allowing the full-range refinement to finish rather than comparing partially converged projects; the monoclinic reference already replays in a few seconds with no bound hits.

The fair triclinic control has now optimized polarization separately and reconverged the full model once. It remains worse (Rwp 2.674% versus 2.392%) and is much less stable (max shift/esd 7.9 versus 0.008). I’m continuing grouped/full replay long enough to ensure this is not merely a starting-point artifact.

The fully re-optimized triclinic alternative remains worse after staged polarization and three full nuisance reconvergences (Rwp 2.598%, still drifting, versus 2.392% and replay-stable for monoclinic). That is enough to reject it without spending more cycles on an ill-conditioned inferior model. I’m now freezing the selected model into `final.gpx` and generating the exact exports.

The final project has been serialized, reopened, and replayed over the full range. It converged with no frozen/bound parameters (max shift/esd 0.0087), Rwp 2.39176%, and crystalline-normalized fractions 51.374% MgWO₄, 41.210% WO₃, and 7.416% MgO. I’m validating the CIF geometry and exact project/CSV/JSON agreement now.

Completed and validated.

Crystalline-normalized phase fractions:

- MgWO₄ (wolframite): **51.37 wt%**
- Monoclinic WO₃: **41.21 wt%**
- MgO (periclase): **7.42 wt%**

Final fit: Rwp 2.392%, Rp 1.824%, GOF 1.569. Realistic fraction uncertainty is approximately 1–2 absolute wt%; amorphous content was not determined.

